];

const faq = [
  { q: 'When do young ravens leave their parents?', a: 'Raven chicks fledge (take their first flight) at approximately 35–42 days after hatching — typically in May or June in temperate North America and Europe. After fledging, the young ravens remain dependent on their parents for a further 4–6 weeks, learning foraging skills and gradually increasing their independence. By late summer (August–September), young ravens from that year\'s brood are largely independent, though they may remain in the loose vicinity of the natal territory for several months. By autumn, they typically join non-breeding flocks of other juveniles and subadults.' },
  { q: 'How long do ravens take to reach breeding age?', a: 'Common Ravens do not breed until they are 3–4 years old on average, though some individuals begin breeding at 2 years old in productive habitats where territories become available. The delay to first breeding reflects the difficulty of obtaining a territory — established breeding pairs hold territories for years, and new breeders can only enter the system when a territory becomes vacant through the death or disappearance of an established bird. The years between fledging and first breeding are spent in non-breeding flocks, developing skills and social knowledge while searching for breeding opportunities.' },
  { q: 'What do young ravens do before they breed?', a: 'Non-breeding young ravens (floaters) form loose flocks with other juveniles and subadults, particularly in winter. These flocks gather at large food sources — carrion, refuse sites, agricultural operations — and form communal roosts at night. The floater period is not merely a waiting period: it is when ravens develop and refine the social and cognitive skills that will serve them as breeding adults. Floaters monitor breeding territories in their area, alert to any vacancy. Some floaters establish brief "trial" pair bonds before obtaining a territory. The skills developed in floater social life — reading other ravens\' behaviour, managing competitive relationships, learning landscape-scale food locations — are directly relevant to later breeding success.' },
];
